  "state_machine_review": [
    "After a frame is returned, the normal loop prioritizes all owned filtered/input packets and the private BSF before considering the safe yield.",
    "The safe point requires private BSF receive EAGAIN and ff_decode_get_packet EAGAIN with no input_pending. FFmpeg7.1.5 ff_decode_get_packet loops over its internal filterchain and transfers buffer_pkt before reporting EAGAIN, so the external input slot can accept a new packet.",
    "Backpressure-required pull and the existing first-frame progress wait can return extra frames. They do not yield while an unsent AU or an internal external-input packet still requires progress.",
    "Once EOS reaches the private BSF, broker draining ignores the batch limit and emits all remaining frames. Flush resets counters and timestamps; first-frame replay64 and retained packet65 ordering remain intact.",
    "The wrapper does not change PTS/DTS/duration selection or Firefox frame dropping policy."
  ],
  "test_coverage": [
    "H264 and HEVC8-frame accumulated ready backlog, each simulated output costs10ms, using the same receive-until-EAGAIN pattern as Firefox.",
    "All returned frames validated for exact ordered PTS/best_effort/pkt_dts/duration and deterministic pixel identities. Every submitted packet retained its original timestamps.",
    "Public send-until-EAGAIN fills FFmpeg buffered frame and input packet slots; every send EAGAIN is followed by at least one received frame before resubmission succeeds.",
    "Broker capacity2 forces5 EAGAIN submissions and extra pulls; normal quota yields to progress, with all24 frames accounted for.",
    "Asynchronous startup accepts64 packets and returns no frame until the third nonzero-timeout pull while packet65 is retained; all70 submissions and outputs remain ordered and unique.",
    "Each case drains exactly to EOF then flushes and decodes3new frames, checking fresh counters and one open/one close.",
    "Occupied-input EOS: send0 prefetches FFmpeg buffer_frame and send1 fills buffer_pkt without entering the stub; sendNULL at that exact state preserves both packets and drains2 exact frames to EOF.",
    "Occupied-input flush: at the same state flush removes both prefetched output and unconsumed packet; receive immediately returns EAGAIN, then a new stream decodes exactly without old output."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "This demonstrates software batching behavior with synthetic compressed AUs and deterministic stub pixels, not hardware60fps acceptance or actual WC copy performance.",
    "One frame is the normal non-draining target, not an unconditional maximum: the forced backpressure case returns6 frames and EOS intentionally drains the remaining64 frames in the startup test.",
    "Residual decoder output may wait for a subsequent submitted packet or explicit drain under this policy; these tests verify no loss and final complete drainage, not zero buffering latency in every caller strategy.",
    "The full native-fallback/real5s timeout/bootstrap/pool regression suite is run separately by desktop_review; the4009 independent checks do not replace it."
  ],
